A comparative accretion-rate bound is a claim of the form: given two objects that can each capture and absorb surrounding matter, if one object's known survival to a certain age implies a lower bound on how slow its absorption process must be, and the other object can be shown (from its physical properties, such as density or state of matter) to absorb matter faster or equally fast under the same triggering process, then the second object must survive for at least as long. Its inputs are the observed lifetime of the first object and a physical comparison of absorption rates between the two objects; its output is an inferred minimum survival time (or maximum absorption rate) for the second object. It applies whenever a hypothesized destructive absorption mechanism is being tested against real-world survival evidence of a comparable but faster-absorbing body.
